WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.290
Not only was Blade a surprise hit for Marvel,
its influence can still be found in some unexpected

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:06.290
places.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:09.769
To honor that legacy, we're looking at how
Blade changed superhero movies, even if you

00:00:09.769 --> 00:00:10.760
didn't always notice.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.119
Marvel characters have been hits on television
since The Marvel Super Heroes cartoon debuted

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:16.119
in 1966.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:18.380
But film success came much harder.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.940
The first feature based on a Marvel comic
was the infamous 1986 flop Howard the Duck,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.860
and things somehow went downhill from there,
with The Punisher and Captain America both

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.780
turned into low budget disasters.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.640
It's enough to make any comic book fan sick.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.480
“Would you please pull over the car, I am
going to get sick."

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.160
"Uh, okay, alright."

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.079
That changed in the 90's, though, thanks to
Blade, which became a certified hit, earning

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:44.350
over $70 million at the domestic box office.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.629
Marvel itself, however, saw very little benefit
from this financial boom.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.679
Struggling to emerge from bankruptcy in the
late 90's, Marvel only pocketed a measly $25,000

00:00:52.679 --> 00:00:53.679
from Blade's success.

00:00:53.679 --> 00:00:55.179
But even that had a silver lining.

00:00:55.179 --> 00:00:59.500
After earning so little from Blade and other
films like X-Men and Spider-Man, Marvel decided

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.499
to make their own film production company
in order to retain all the profits from their

00:01:03.499 --> 00:01:04.499
characters.

00:01:04.499 --> 00:01:08.080
The result was the Marvel Cinematic Universe,
which may never have existed without Blade.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.280
Writer David S. Goyer has been behind some
of the biggest superhero films ever, with

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.830
writing credits on the entire Dark Knight
Trilogy, Man of Steel, and Batman v. Superman:

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:17.830
Dawn of Justice.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.470
But he may never have had the chance to do
those films if not for Blade.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:24.810
"I wrote a script called Blade, and that script
changed my career completely.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:29.640
For the first time, I wrote a script without
any editorial interference.”

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.780
It worked, and Goyer went on two write all
three films in the Blade Trilogy, even directing

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.800
the final installment, Blade: Trinity.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.360
"Take a look at this."

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.120
"You gotta be kidding me."

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.800
Gloomy vibes.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:47.820
Night creatures.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.820
Black leather.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.770
Goth was big in the 1980's, but by the 90's,
casual grunge styles, hip-hop culture, and

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.640
bright pop colors ruled the day.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.810
Until Blade, that is!

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.810
Blade helped usher in a new wave of darker
fare, with a sleek cinematic style that informed

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.270
both superhero films and Hollywood in general.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.100
One doesn't need heightened senses to notice
the direct line from Blade to the zombie action

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.500
of Resident Evil or the vampire vs. werewolf
battles of the Underworld franchise.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.730
Even The Matrix follows in Snipes' leather-clad
footsteps, both in its shiny black aesthetic

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.120
and in its ideas of an underworld waging a
war that humans can't see.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.160
The tagline for the 1978 Superman movie starring
Christopher Reeve boldly declared, "You'll

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.400
believe a man can fly."

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.400
We did.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.060
But it also helped that everybody in the entire
world already knew who Superman was.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.740
And the same was true of the 1989 mega-hit
Batman.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.390
“I want you to tell all your friends about
me."

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.730
"What are you?"

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.300
"I'm Batman!"

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.530
With other comic book films floundering, conventional
wisdom in Hollywood was that only the biggest

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.959
and most famous superheroes could be successful
at the box office.

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:47.050
Blade changed all that.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:50.980
An obscure cult character even among comic
book fans, the success of the film proved

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.220
that even c-list superheroes could be box
office gold if the films were done right.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.180
So if you liked Guardians of the Galaxy or
Doctor Strange, you can thank Blade for blazing

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.080
the trail.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.070
Hollywood also had another bit of conventional
wisdom that proved to be a bunch of crap:

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.240
the idea that comic book movies were for kids.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.280
Sure, Tim Burton's Batman had a darker aesthetic,
but even that quickly made way for nipple

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.320
suits and bad puns.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.480
“What killed the dinosaurs?

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.400
The ice age!"

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.000
Comic book fans, though, knew that there was
a lot more depth in the source material.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.610
There's a reason DC had to start putting a
"mature readers" label on some of their comics

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:28.860
in the late 80's.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.360
But until Blade, the film world felt that
comic book movies were just silly stuff for

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:33.120
kids.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.800
“Freeze!”

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.960
“Motherf----r, are you out of your damn
mind?!"

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.830
Blade brought a darker, more grounded sense
of realism and violence to comic book films.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:48.290
It was the first character from the "Big Two"
publishers, Marvel and DC, to arrive in American

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:49.910
theaters with an R rating.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:54.000
And its success paved the way not only for
more serious superhero treatments in mainstream

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.310
film, but also for a wave of R-rated comic
book films, from Sin City to Deadpool to Logan.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.300
Even Marvel's Netflix shows owe Blade a debt:
look again at Daredevil's hard-hitting punches,

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.819
and you might spot a resemblance to Blade's
bloodsucking melees.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:08.020
Thank you, Blade!

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.239
